// Functions.
//
// Written against #165. Every snippet and every transcript below was run
// against the `wcl` CLI before it was written down; the four older
// concept units (function_literals, fn_items, function_types,
// higher_order_functions) were re-cut against the parser and the
// evaluator rather than lifted.
page lang_functions {
  title = "Functions"

  chapter_header "Functions" {
    kicker       = "The WCL language · reference"
    reading_time = "15 min read"
    updated      = "2026-08-20"
    version      = "wcl 0.33.2-alpha"
  }

  p "A function in WCL is a **value**, the same way a number or a list is a value. You write one with `fn`, bind it to a name or hand it straight to another function, and call it with parentheses. There is one calling convention and it is deliberately small: one parameter list, filled left to right, every time. No default arguments, no named arguments, no overloading."

  p "This chapter covers the literal, the `fn` item that names one, the type that describes one, and the builtins that call one. It also covers what a function can see while it runs. It ends with the file the whole chapter builds towards."

  p "Every file and every command below was run before it was written down. Type them and compare."

  h2 "A function is a value"

  p "The literal is `fn(params) -> ReturnType body`. Each parameter is `name: Type`. The return type follows `->`. The body is one expression:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
let double = fn(x: i64) -> i64 x * 2i64
WCL
  }

  p "Nothing on that line is a declaration. `fn(x: i64) -> i64 x * 2i64` is an expression that evaluates to a function value, exactly as `[1, 2]` evaluates to a list. The `let` is the ordinary [`let` item](lang_documents#let-items), and it does nothing special."

  p "The body may also be a `{ … }` **block expression**: local bindings, then one final expression that is the value. Note the semicolon: the expression-level `let` inside braces takes one, and the `let` item outside does not. [Expressions and operators](lang_expressions) covers both."

  code wcl {
    filename = "arith.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@document
type Cfg {
  four: i64
  nine: i64
}

let double = fn(x: i64) -> i64 x * 2i64
let sum_sq = fn(x: i64, y: i64) -> i64 { let s = x + y; s * s }

four = double(2i64)
nine = sum_sq(1i64, 2i64)
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check arith.wcl
OK
$ wcl get arith.wcl four
4
$ wcl get arith.wcl nine
9
CONSOLE
  }

  p "A parameter list may be empty, and it may carry one trailing comma:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
let answer = fn() -> i64 42i64
let add    = fn(x: i64, y: i64,) -> i64 x + y
WCL
  }

  callout "Both annotations are mandatory" {
    kind = :note
    body  = "WCL infers neither end of a signature. `fn(x) -> i64 x` fails with `expected ':' after parameter name, found ')'`, and `fn(x: i64) x` fails with `expected '->' before return type`. Every parameter carries a type and every function states its return type, in a literal and in an item alike."
  }

  h2 "fn items"

  p "Write `fn` **before** a name, at file or block scope, to bind the same literal to that name:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
fn add(x: i64, y: i64) -> i64 x + y

# The same binding, spelled the other way.
let add2 = fn(x: i64, y: i64) -> i64 x + y
WCL
  }

  p "The two lines are one item form. [Documents, fields and blocks](lang_documents#the-thirteen-item-forms) counts thirteen item forms and fourteen spellings for exactly this reason. A `fn` item **is** a `let` item: the same node, the same resolution path."

  h3 "let items as composition helpers"

  p "So everything [`let` items](lang_documents#let-items) says applies to a `fn` item unchanged, and the part that matters most is what a `let` is _for_. A `let` binds a name that sibling and descendant expressions may read, and nothing else can see at all. It helps you write the data. It is not data. Reach for it when only other expressions need the value. Reach for a field when a consumer of the document is meant to read it."

  p "A function is the purest case of that. It means nothing to a consumer reading the file back. So the natural place to put one is a binding that leaves no trace. Both spellings are invisible to `wcl get`, to `wcl parse` output, to a JSON serialization, and to schema validation:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "calc.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@document
type Cfg { total: i64 }

fn add(x: i64, y: i64) -> i64 x + y

total = add(10i64, 20i64)
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get calc.wcl total
30
$ wcl get calc.wcl add
no such path: add
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The one function that _is_ data is the one a field holds, and that is [a function type](#function-types), further down."

  h3 "What a fn item adds"

  p "A `fn` item adds exactly two things over `let name = fn(…)`."

  p "**It is indexed.** The parser deliberately keeps a plain `let` out of the symbol index. That is what stops it leaking into a document's field list, or into `wcl get`. A `fn` item opts back in, as a function declaration. The language server reads that index. A `fn` item, and only a `fn` item, gets an outline entry, a hover card, and go-to-definition. See [The CLI](lang_cli) for `wcl lsp`."

  p "The index has a second, sharper effect: **it catches a duplicate name.** Two `fn` items sharing a name are a parse error. Two `let` bindings sharing a name are not, and the first one silently wins."

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check dup-fn.wcl
wcl::parse

  × duplicate declaration 'f'
   ╭─[dup-fn.wcl:3:1]
 2 │ type Cfg { a: i64 }
 3 │ fn f(x: i64) -> i64 1i64
   · ────────────┬───────────
   ·             ╰── first declared here
 4 │ fn f(x: i64) -> i64 2i64
   · ────────────┬───────────
   ·             ╰── duplicate declaration
 5 │ a = f(1i64)
   ╰────

$ wcl check dup-let.wcl
OK
$ wcl get dup-let.wcl a
1
CONSOLE
  }

  p "**It takes decorators.** A `let` refuses them outright. A `fn` item accepts them, and the decorator system knows `fn` as a position of its own, alongside `field`, `block`, and `type`."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
@doc("Trim a label and lower-case it.")
fn tidy(s: utf8) -> utf8 to_lower(trim(s))
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check decorated-let.wcl
wcl::parse

  × decorators are not allowed on let bindings
   ╭─[decorated-let.wcl:3:1]
 2 │ type Cfg { a: i64 }
 3 │ @doc("x")
   · ────┬────
   ·     ╰── remove decorator
 4 │ let f = fn(x: i64) -> i64 x
   ╰────
CONSOLE
  }

  p "[Decorators](lang_decorators) covers what you may write there. So reach for `fn name(…)` for anything you want to find again. Reach for `let name = fn(…)` when the function is a one-off you need twice."

  h3 "Where a fn item may sit"

  p "Anywhere a `let` item may sit: the top level of a file, or inside a block. Inside a block, the item's scope is that block and its descendants:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "ports.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  port:  u32
  admin: u32
}

@document
type Cfg {
  @children("service") services: list<Service>
}

service web {
  fn bump(p: u32) -> u32 p + 1u32

  port  = 8080u32
  admin = bump(self.port)
}
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get ports.wcl services.web.admin
8081u32
$ wcl get ports.wcl services.web.bump
no such path: services.web.bump
did you mean: service?
CONSOLE
  }

  p "Recursion needs nothing special. A name at document scope resolves when the call runs, not when you write the body, so a function may name itself:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "fact.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@document
type Cfg { a: i64 }

fn fact(n: i64) -> i64 if n <= 1i64 { 1i64 } else { n * fact(n - 1i64) }

a = fact(5i64)
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get fact.wcl a
120
CONSOLE
  }

  p "That laziness is the same one [How a document evaluates](lang_evaluation) describes for fields, and it is why item order never matters. There is a ceiling: 256 nested calls, after which evaluation stops with `call depth limit exceeded (max 256)`."

  h2 "Function types"

  p "`fn(T1, T2, …) -> R` is a **type**, and it describes a callable. Note how it differs from the literal. A type names the parameter types only, because a parameter's name is no part of a caller's contract."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Written"                      | "Is"      | "Means" |
      | "`fn(x: i64) -> i64 x * 2i64`" | "a value" | "this function" |
      | "`fn(i64) -> i64`"             | "a type"  | "some function taking an `i64` and returning an `i64`" |
  }

  p "Use it wherever a type goes: a field on a block, an element of a `list<…>`, or a parameter of another function. A field declared that way holds a real function, and you call it straight through the dotted path:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "steps.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("step")
type Step {
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  apply: fn(i64) -> i64
}

@document
type Cfg {
  @children("step") steps: list<Step>
  out: i64
}

step scale {
  apply = fn(x: i64) -> i64 x * 3i64
}

out = steps.scale.apply(7i64)
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check steps.wcl
OK
$ wcl get steps.wcl out
21
$ wcl get steps.wcl steps.scale.apply
fn(x: i64) -> i64 { ... }
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The schema checks that field the way it checks any other. Write `apply = 3i64` instead and `wcl check` names it:"

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check steps.wcl
wcl::eval::schema_violation

  × field 'apply' declared as fn(i64) -> i64 but value is i64

steps.wcl: 1 schema violation
CONSOLE
  }

  callout "A function value is not serializable data" {
    kind = :warning
    body  = "The check above is a shape check, and it is where the guarantees stop. A function value has no JSON form: `wcl get --json steps.wcl steps.scale.apply` prints `null`, not an error. Declare a `fn` field when a **host**, or a later expression in the same document, will call it. Do not declare one on data you expect to round-trip through JSON."
  }

  h2 "Higher-order functions"

  p "A function that takes or returns another function is higher-order. WCL needs nothing extra for it: a function is a value, so it passes and returns like one. That is how the collection builtins take their behaviour from you."

  code wcl {
    filename = "hof.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@document
type Cfg {
  doubled: list<i64>
  evens:   list<i64>
  total:   i64
}

doubled = map([1i64, 2i64, 3i64], fn(x: i64) -> i64 x * 2i64)
evens   = filter([1i64, 2i64, 3i64, 4i64], fn(x: i64) -> bool x % 2i64 == 0i64)
total   = fold([1i64, 2i64, 3i64], 0i64, fn(acc: i64, x: i64) -> i64 acc + x)
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get hof.wcl doubled
[2, 4, 6]
$ wcl get hof.wcl evens
[2, 4]
$ wcl get hof.wcl total
6
CONSOLE
  }

  p "Eleven builtins **call** the function you hand them. Learn these by heart:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Builtin"             | "The function it takes" | "Gives back" |
      | "`map(xs, f)`"        | "`fn(T) -> U`"          | "`[U]`, every element transformed" |
      | "`filter(xs, pred)`"  | "`fn(T) -> bool`"       | "`[T]`, the elements that passed" |
      | "`fold(xs, init, f)`" | "`fn(U, T) -> U`"       | "`U`, one accumulated value" |
      | "`any(xs, pred)`"     | "`fn(T) -> bool`"       | "`bool`, true for at least one (short-circuits)" |
      | "`all(xs, pred)`"     | "`fn(T) -> bool`"       | "`bool`, true for every one (true when empty)" |
      | "`find(xs, pred)`"    | "`fn(T) -> bool`"       | "the first match, or `none`" |
      | "`sort_by(xs, key)`"  | "`fn(T) -> K`"          | "`[T]`, ascending by key, stable" |
      | "`min_by(xs, key)`"   | "`fn(T) -> K`"          | "the element with the smallest key, or `none`" |
      | "`max_by(xs, key)`"   | "`fn(T) -> K`"          | "the element with the largest key, or `none`" |
      | "`group_by(xs, key)`" | "`fn(T) -> K`"          | "`[{ key, items }]`, in first-seen key order" |
      | "`map_values(r, f)`"  | "`fn(T) -> U`"          | "a record, same keys, transformed values" |
  }

  p "[Builtins](lang_builtins) documents each in full, and [Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections) covers the collections they walk. They compose the way you would expect. A **named** function goes in by name, with no wrapper and no call parentheses:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "words.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@document
type Cfg {
  shortest_first: utf8
  slugs:          list<utf8>
}

fn slug(s: utf8) -> utf8 replace(to_lower(s), " ", "-")

shortest_first = join(sort_by(["pear", "fig", "apple"], fn(s: utf8) -> i64 len(s)), ", ")
slugs          = map(["Hello World", "Bye Now"], slug)
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get words.wcl shortest_first
"fig, pear, apple"
$ wcl get words.wcl slugs
["hello-world", "bye-now"]
CONSOLE
  }

  callout "A builtin is not a value" {
    kind = :warning
    body  = "`map(xs, slug)` works because `slug` is a `fn` item, and a `fn` item is a binding that holds a function value. A **builtin** is not a binding: `map([\"x\", \"y\"], to_upper)` fails with `unresolved reference 'to_upper'`. Builtins are callable by name, not passable by name. Wrap one in a literal to pass it: `map([\"x\", \"y\"], fn(s: utf8) -> utf8 to_upper(s))`."
  }

  h3 "Returning a function"

  p "To curry, give a literal a return type that is itself a function type:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "curry.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@document
type Cfg { seven: i64 }

let adder = fn(x: i64) -> fn(i64) -> i64 fn(y: i64) -> i64 x + y
let add3  = adder(3i64)

seven = add3(4i64)
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get curry.wcl seven
7
CONSOLE
  }

  p "Read that middle line in three parts. `fn(x: i64)` is the outer parameter list. `-> fn(i64) -> i64` is the outer return **type**. The rest is an inner literal, and it is the outer body. `add3` is that inner function with `x` already fixed at 3. It is a closure, and closures are the next section."

  h2 "Closures and capture"

  p "A function literal captures the **local bindings** in scope where you write the literal. It captures them by value, at the moment WCL evaluates the literal:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "capture.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@document
type Cfg { a: i64 }

# `f` captures n = 1. Re-binding `n` afterwards changes nothing.
a = { let n = 1i64; let f = fn(x: i64) -> i64 x + n; let n = 100i64; f(0i64) }
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get capture.wcl a
1
CONSOLE
  }

  p "A function literal does not capture names at **document** scope, and does not need to. A document is immutable once it is open. A `let` item, a field, or a block therefore resolves by a scope walk at call time. That is why the recursive `fact` above works, and why a function may read a field declared below it."

  p "Inside a call, names resolve in this order. The last binding of a name wins:"

  list {
    li "**The captures**, pushed first."
    li "**The parameters**, pushed second. A parameter shadows a capture of the same name."
    li "**Whatever the body binds itself**, in a `{ … }` block expression, a `match` arm or an `if let`."
    li "**Document scope**, walked outward from the block the call sits in, for any name none of the above bound."
  }

  callout "Close over what you use" {
    kind = :warning
    body  = "A **free name** is one the function neither captured nor took as a parameter. The call site resolves it, not the place you wrote the function. A function whose body names `ghost` picks up whatever `ghost` the **caller** holds in a local binding. At the next call site, which has none, it fails. Nothing warns you. Write functions that use their parameters, their captures, and document-scope names. Treat anything else as a bug you have not hit yet."
  }

  h2 "No defaults and no named arguments"

  p "A parameter list is fixed at declaration. Three things you may reach for do not exist, and each is a **parse** error rather than a surprise at run time."

  p "There are no **optional** parameters. `?` marks an optional field in a type declaration. In a parameter list it does not parse:"

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check optional.wcl
wcl::parse

  × expected ',' or ')' in parameter list, found '?'
   ╭─[optional.wcl:3:20]
 2 │ type Cfg { a: utf8 }
 3 │ fn greet(name: utf8?) -> utf8 "hi"
   ·                    ┬
   ·                    ╰── expected ',' or ')'
 4 │ a = greet("x")
   ╰────
CONSOLE
  }

  p "There are no **default** arguments:"

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check default.wcl
wcl::parse

  × expected ',' or ')' in parameter list, found '='
   ╭─[default.wcl:3:21]
 2 │ type Cfg { a: utf8 }
 3 │ fn greet(name: utf8 = "world") -> utf8 name
   ·                     ┬
   ·                     ╰── expected ',' or ')'
 4 │ a = greet("x")
   ╰────
CONSOLE
  }

  p "And there are no **named** arguments at the call site. `greet(name = \"x\")` fails too, on the `=` rather than the `:`:"

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check named.wcl
wcl::parse

  × expected ',' or ')' in call arguments, found ':'
   ╭─[named.wcl:4:15]
 3 │ fn greet(name: utf8) -> utf8 name
 4 │ a = greet(name: "x")
   ·               ┬
   ·               ╰── expected ',' or ')'
   ╰────
CONSOLE
  }

  p "So every call fills every parameter, positionally. What the language checks of that call is exactly one thing, **the count**:"

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get arity.wcl a
wcl::eval::builtin_arity

  × 'add' expects 2 argument(s), got 1
CONSOLE
  }

  p "It does not check argument **types**. Pass a `utf8` where the signature says `i64` and the call evaluates cleanly, with the wrong value flowing on. Nor does it check the return type: `fn f(x: i64) -> utf8 x + 1i64` returns the number 2, and the mismatch surfaces only later, when a field declared `utf8` receives it. A signature is a contract you are keeping, not one the evaluator enforces for you."

  h3 "What a fixed parameter list costs"

  p "Arity is the one positional mistake WCL catches, and a fixed parameter list is the one shape a caller can rely on. The wdoc standard library is where that trade-off is most visible. Its `el` family builds the HTML element vocabulary, and it is three constructors rather than one **because** a parameter list cannot vary:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
el(tag, cls, kids)           # the common shape
ela(tag, cls, attrs, kids)   # … with attributes
eli(tag, id, cls, kids)      # … with an explicit HTML id
WCL
  }

  p "Two consequences follow, and both are worth copying when you write a constructor family of your own. First, an optional value goes in as an ordinary argument. `?` cannot appear in a parameter list, so every wdoc function annotates its optionals as though they were required, and lets the `none` flow through. Second, `ela` and `eli` take the **same** number of arguments, so arity cannot separate them. Call one where you meant the other and it silently drops the id, or the attrs. Pick by what you are passing. [Documents, pages and sites](wdoc_sites) documents the family. [Writing your own blocks](wdoc_extending) is where you will call it."

  h2 "Four ways to write a function down"

  p "The forms above are not interchangeable. All four hold the same kind of value, and they differ in what the rest of the system can see of it."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Form"                     | "Named" | "In the symbol index" | "Takes decorators" | "Document data" | "Reach for it when" |
      | "Literal at the call site" | "No"    | "No"                  | "No"               | "No"            | "The function is an argument and nothing else" |
      | "`let f = fn(…)`"          | "Yes"   | "No"                  | "No"               | "No"            | "A one-off you happen to need twice" |
      | "`fn f(…)`"                | "Yes"   | "Yes"                 | "Yes"              | "No"            | "Anything you want to find again" |
      | "A `fn`-typed field"       | "Yes"   | "As a field"          | "As a field"       | "**Yes**"       | "The document is _configuring_ behaviour" |
  }

  p "The fourth row is the one the others cannot substitute for. A `let` and a `fn` item help you write the data, then vanish. A `fn`-typed field **is** data: schema-checked, addressable by path, part of what a host reads back. That is the same distinction [Documents, fields and blocks](lang_documents) draws between a field and a `let`, and it holds for functions exactly as it holds for numbers."

  h2 "A worked example"

  p "One file uses every form. Save it as `rollout.wcl`:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "rollout.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
# rollout.wcl — three services, and the capacity plan derived from them.

@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  tier:     utf8
  replicas: u32
  cpu:      f64
}

@block("plan")
type Plan {
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  # A function is a value, so a field may hold one.
  headroom: fn(f64) -> f64
  budget:   f64
}

@document
type Cfg {
  @children("service") services: list<Service>
  @child("plan")       plan:     Plan
  requested:   f64
  provisioned: f64
  core:        list<identifier>
  fits:        bool
}

# A `fn` item: named once, called anywhere, invisible to the document.
fn demand(s: Service) -> f64 s.cpu * s.replicas

# A `let` item — the closure below captures it.
let core_tier = "core"

service web { tier = "edge"  replicas = 3u32  cpu = 0.5 }
service api { tier = "core"  replicas = 2u32  cpu = 1.5 }
service db  { tier = "core"  replicas = 1u32  cpu = 4.0 }

plan q3 {
  headroom = fn(cpu: f64) -> f64 cpu * 1.25
  budget   = 12.0
}

requested   = fold(services, 0.0, fn(acc: f64, s: Service) -> f64 acc + demand(s))
provisioned = plan.headroom(requested)
core        = map(filter(services, fn(s: Service) -> bool s.tier == core_tier),
                  fn(s: Service) -> identifier s.name)
fits        = provisioned <= plan.budget
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check rollout.wcl
OK
$ wcl get rollout.wcl requested
8.5
$ wcl get rollout.wcl provisioned
10.625
$ wcl get rollout.wcl core
[api, db]
$ wcl get rollout.wcl fits
true
CONSOLE
  }

  p "Four functions, four forms, one file:"

  list {
    li "`demand` is a **`fn` item**. It takes a whole `Service` block as its argument, since a block is a value like any other, and the `fold` reuses it."
    li "`headroom` is a **`fn`-typed field** on the `plan` block. The document does not merely describe the plan. It carries the plan's arithmetic, and `provisioned = plan.headroom(requested)` calls it through the ordinary dotted path."
    li "The three literals handed to `fold`, `filter`, and `map` are **anonymous**. Nothing else needs them, so nothing else names them."
    li "The predicate `filter` gets is a **closure**: it reads `core_tier`, which is a `let` item, not a parameter."
  }

  p "Change `budget` to `10.0` and `fits` flips to `false`. The document recomputes because nothing was ever precomputed. [How a document evaluates](lang_evaluation) is that story in full."

  h2 "What a function does not do"

  p "The gaps below are the language as it stands, not oversights to be patched around. Each has already appeared above. Here they are in one place."

  list {
    li "**No overloading.** One name, one function. Two `fn` items sharing a name is `duplicate declaration`."
    li "**No default or named arguments, and no optional parameters.** Every call fills every parameter, in order. See [above](#no-defaults-and-no-named-arguments)."
    li "**No generics.** There is no `fn name<T>(…)` form. `fn f<T>(x: i64) -> i64 x` fails with `expected '(' after fn name`. A signature names concrete types. The higher-order builtins take any type because Rust implements them, not because you could write one."
    li "**No argument or return type checking.** WCL checks the argument count and nothing else. The annotations are documentation, and the [schema](lang_schemas) catches the wrong value in the end."
    li "**Builtins are not values.** Wrap one in a literal to pass it."
    li "**No serialization.** A function value is `null` in JSON, and `fn(x: i64) -> i64 { ... }` in `wcl get`."
    li "**A depth ceiling.** 256 nested calls, then `call depth limit exceeded (max 256)`."
  }

  h2 "Where to go next"

  list {
    li "[Expressions and operators](lang_expressions). The grammar you write a body in, including the `{ … }` block expression and its `let … ;` bindings."
    li "[Control flow](lang_control_flow). `if`, `if let`, `match`, `try`, and `catch`. All of them are expressions, so all of them are legal as a function body."
    li "[Types](lang_types). Where `fn(T) -> R` sits among the other type forms."
    li "[Builtins](lang_builtins). The eleven higher-order builtins in full, plus everything else callable by name."
    li "[Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections). The collections `map`, `filter`, and `fold` walk."
    li "[How a document evaluates](lang_evaluation). Laziness, caching, and cycle detection, and why a document-scope name resolves at call time."
    li "[Namespaces and imports](lang_namespaces). A `fn` item in an imported file is callable by its bare name."
    li "[Writing your own blocks](wdoc_extending). Where writing functions stops being incidental and becomes the job: a wdoc block is a `lower` function returning content."
  }
}
